---
import Docs from "../../layouts/Docs.astro";
import CodeBlock from "../../components/CodeBlock.astro";
import {
  SheetPlayground,
  SheetSides,
  SheetSizes,
  SheetSwipe,
} from "../../components/SheetDemo";
import { SheetPatterns, SheetStates } from "../../components/SheetPatternsDemo";
---

<Docs
  title="Sheet"
  description="Panels that slide in from an edge and swipe closed, for details, filters, editors, and navigation."
>
  <h1 class="docs-title">Sheet</h1>
  <p class="docs-lead">
    A modal panel that slides in from an edge of the screen. Use it for details,
    filters, and editors that need more room than a dialog but should keep the
    page behind in view. Focus stays inside until it closes. On a touch screen,
    swipe it back toward its edge to close it.
  </p>
  <div class="mt-8" id="sheet-demo"><SheetPlayground client:load /></div>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Usage</h2>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Sheet usage"
      code={`import { Button } from "@tulipfarm/sprout/button";
import {
  Sheet,
  SheetBody,
  SheetContent,
  SheetDescription,
  SheetFooter,
  SheetHeader,
  SheetTitle,
  SheetTrigger,
} from "@tulipfarm/sprout/sheet";

<Sheet open={open} onOpenChange={setOpen}>
  <SheetTrigger render={<Button variant="outline" size="sm" />}>
    <Filter aria-hidden="true" />
    Filters (2)
  </SheetTrigger>
  <SheetContent side="bottom">
    <SheetHeader>
      <SheetTitle>Filter and sort</SheetTitle>
      <SheetDescription>Results update as you choose.</SheetDescription>
    </SheetHeader>
    <SheetBody>…</SheetBody>
    <SheetFooter>
      <Button onClick={() => setOpen(false)}>Show results</Button>
      <Button variant="ghost" size="sm" onClick={clear}>Clear filters</Button>
    </SheetFooter>
  </SheetContent>
</Sheet>`}
    />
    <p>
      <code>Sheet</code> works controlled with <code>open</code> and <code
        >onOpenChange</code
      >, or uncontrolled with a <code>SheetTrigger</code>. To keep it open while
      a save runs or while a second sheet is on top, ignore the change in <code
        >onOpenChange</code
      > and hide the corner button with <code
        >showCloseButton={"{!saving}"}</code
      >.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Sides</h2>
    <p>
      <code>side</code> is <code>"right"</code> (default), <code>"left"</code>,
      <code>"top"</code>, or <code>"bottom"</code>. Top and bottom sheets span
      the width, grow with their content up to 85% of the height, and lay out
      footer actions in a row from 640px.
    </p>
    <SheetSides client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Sizes</h2>
    <p>
      <code>size</code> sets the width of left and right sheets: <code
        >"default"</code
      > (24rem), <code>"lg"</code> (32rem), or <code>"xl"</code> (42rem). <code
        >"full"</code
      > covers the viewport from any side and respects safe areas. <code
        >SheetTitle size="lg"</code
      > is the larger title for product-style detail sheets.
    </p>
    <SheetSizes client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section" id="sheet-swipe">
    <h2 class="section-title">Swipe to close</h2>
    <p>
      Every sheet follows a touch swipe toward its <code>side</code>. A right
      sheet swipes right and a bottom sheet swipes down. Let go past half the
      panel, or flick it, and the sheet closes. A shorter swipe springs back.
      Scrolling a long body scrolls first and swipes only from the edge.
    </p>
    <p>
      A mouse or pen drags the sheet only from the handle or empty panel space.
      Drags on text, fields, and buttons select and click as usual, so desktop
      forms are safe. <code>showSwipeHandle</code> adds a grab bar on the edge facing
      the page. It is off by default. Turn it on for bottom sheets on phones, where
      people expect it.
    </p>
    <CodeBlock
      lang="tsx"
      title="Bottom sheet with a handle"
      code={`<Sheet>
  <SheetTrigger render={<Button variant="outline" />}>Stock filters</SheetTrigger>
  <SheetContent side="bottom" showSwipeHandle>
    <SheetHeader>
      <SheetTitle>Stock filters</SheetTitle>
      <SheetDescription>Swipe down or press Show results to close.</SheetDescription>
    </SheetHeader>
    <SheetBody>…</SheetBody>
    <SheetFooter>
      <SheetClose render={<Button />}>Show results</SheetClose>
    </SheetFooter>
  </SheetContent>
</Sheet>`}
    />
    <SheetSwipe client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Cloud patterns</h2>
    <p>
      Put a custom bar and your own scroll region directly in <code
        >SheetContent</code
      >, or use <code>SheetBody</code>, which scrolls between the header and
      footer. <code>initialFocus</code> can target a title with <code
        >tabIndex={"{-1}"}</code
      >, <code>finalFocus</code> can return focus to a trigger ref, and <code
        >keepMounted</code
      > keeps drafts while the sheet is closed. A dialog or a second sheet can open
      on top.
    </p>
    <SheetPatterns client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">States</h2>
    <p>
      Show a skeleton with <code>role="status"</code> while data loads, an error
      <code>StatePanel</code> with a retry, and a short sentence when nothing needs
      attention.
    </p>
    <SheetStates client:load />
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section" id="sheet-api">
    <h2 class="section-title">API</h2>
    <p>
      <code>Sheet</code>, <code>SheetTrigger</code>, <code>SheetPortal</code>,
      <code>SheetClose</code>, <code>SheetOverlay</code>, <code>SheetTitle</code
      >, and <code>SheetDescription</code> wrap the Base UI drawer parts and take
      their props, including <code>render</code>. <code>SheetHeader</code>, <code
        >SheetBody</code
      >, and <code>SheetFooter</code> render a <code>div</code> and accept <code
        >render</code
      >, so a body can be the form. <code>Sheet</code> leaves out <code
        >swipeDirection</code
      > and snap points. The <code>side</code> on <code>SheetContent</code> sets the
      swipe direction.
    </p>
    <p>
      <code>SheetContent</code> takes <code>side</code>, <code>size</code>, <code
        >showCloseButton</code
      >, <code>closeLabel</code>, <code>keepMounted</code>, <code
        >container</code
      >, <code>showSwipeHandle</code>, and every Base UI popup prop such as <code
        >initialFocus</code
      > and <code>finalFocus</code>. It sets <code
        >data-slot="sheet-content"</code
      > (overridable), <code>data-side</code>, and <code>data-size</code>. Its
      corner button has <code>data-slot="sheet-close"</code>, so you can move it
      with a <code>className</code> selector. <code>SheetTitle</code> takes <code
        >size</code
      > (<code>"default"</code> or <code>"lg"</code>).
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Accessibility</h2>
    <p>
      The panel has <code>role="dialog"</code> and is named by <code
        >SheetTitle</code
      >. Every sheet needs a title, even if it is hidden with <code
        >sr-only</code
      >. Tab and Shift+Tab stay inside, and Escape or a click on the overlay
      closes it unless you ignore the change.
    </p>
    <p>
      Opening slides 2.5rem and fades over 180ms. Closing takes 120ms. A swipe
      moves the panel with the finger, and the overlay fades as it goes. Motion
      turns off when reduced motion is requested. The swipe still works. The
      handle is hidden from assistive tech, so keep a close button or a footer
      action in every sheet.
    </p>
  </section>
  <section class="prose-section">
    <h2 class="section-title">Theme tokens</h2>
    <p>
      Sheets use <code>--sprout-overlay</code>, <code>--sprout-surface</code>, <code
        >--sprout-line</code
      >, <code>--sprout-text</code>, <code>--sprout-muted</code>, <code
        >--sprout-shadow-overlay</code
      >, and <code>--sprout-radius-panel</code>. They need no brand color, so
      they look the same with the neutral and TulipFarm themes in light and dark
      mode.
    </p>
  </section>
</Docs>
